  </ctml:games>
  <ctml:notes>2nd Norway Chess Women 2025 (Stavanger, Norway, 26 May - 6 June 2025): a 6-player DOUBLE round robin over 10 rounds, 3 boards per round, under the Norway Chess scoring system. A decisive classical game (120 min for the whole game with a 10 s/move increment from move 41) scores 3-0. A DRAWN classical game is followed immediately by an armageddon on the same board - White 10 minutes, Black 7 minutes with draw odds - worth 1.5 to its winner and 1 to its loser, so a drawn classical pairing yields 2.5 points between the two players rather than 1. Both cadences are one event here (header cadence "mixed"): participant/score carries the official Norway points and participant/placement the official finishing order; each participant's classical-only crosstable score and armageddon record are in its notes. Games are tagged by cadence - game/@round "1"-"10" classical, "A1"-"A10" armageddon, with the armageddon carrying the same board number as the drawn classical game it settled, plus game/timeControl - so both tables recompute from the 48 games. The builder asserts the official points, the games-played column, and the classical crosstable before emitting. Anna Muzychuk won the second edition of Norway Chess Women on 16.5 points, half a point clear of Lei Tingjie after drawing her final classical game with Vaishali. Defending champion Ju Wenjun finished fourth. Muzychuk and Lei tied the classical-only crosstable on 6/10; the armageddon points separated them in the official table. Final standings: 1 Muzychuk, Anna 16.5, 2 Lei, Tingjie 16, 3 Koneru, Humpy 15, 4 Ju, Wenjun 13.5, 5 Vaishali, Rameshbabu 11, 6 Khademalsharieh, Sarasadat 9. The source numbers rounds 1-20 interleaved (odd classical, even armageddon); the split is cross-checked against the source TimeControl tag (every armageddon carries "600+0", every classical "40/7200:0+10") and against the opening clocks (every armageddon game starts 600s/420s and no classical game does), and the 3/1.5/1/0 scoring reproduces the published points. Unlike the 2026 edition, TWIC did not publish separately split classical/armageddon files for 2025, so that further cross-check is not available here. ECO codes are classified by longest UCI-prefix match (no ECO headers in the source). Every ply has a clock value. Identity is by FIDE id. The source PGN carries no federation, so each player's is resolved by FIDE id against the official FIDE standard rating list and cross-checked against the Wikipedia final standings table; the builder refuses to emit if the two disagree. Ratings in participant/ratingSnapshot are the classical Elo carried on the classical games (the armageddon games carry a separate blitz rating and are ignored for the event starting Elo). No engine evaluations were present in the source, so none are invented. Uses the CTML 2.1 model (participant/placement).</ctml:notes>
  <ctml:source kind="twic-pgn">
    <ctml:uri>file:///D:/elysium/sources/twic/2025-norway-chess-women.pgn</ctml:uri>
    <ctml:note>48 games (30 classical + 18 armageddon); SHA-256 089bb1b5b6f9b95dc3f10e458013b3fa179307464bb313de81af920b2061704a. Moves, clocks, FIDE ids, TimeControl tags and results authoritative.</ctml:note>
  </ctml:source>
